# text = I shall say that he is mistaken in supposing that I can regret the birth of my daughters ( who have been the pride of my life , and are likely to be the comfort of my old age ), or the thirty years I have passed in the company of my best and dearest friend ;-- that , had our misfortunes been three times as great as they were ( unless they had been of my bringing on ), I should still the more rejoice to have shared them with your father , and administered what consolation I was able ; and , had his sufferings in illness been ten times what they wore , I could not regret having watched over and laboured to relieve them ;-- that , if he had married a richer wife , misfortunes and trials would no doubt have come upon him still ; while I am egotist enough to imagine that no other woman could have cheered him through them so well : not that I am superior to the rest , but I was made for him , and he for me ; and I can no more repent the hours , days , years of happiness we have spent together , and which neither could have had without the other , than I can the privilege of having been his nurse in sickness , and his comfort in affliction
# sent_id = 865
